tel 1.0

base cosmopolite
import soundness

info   multilingual values whose types record their languages, locales, and each language's sort order
detail
    _Cosmopolite_ puts a program's translations in its code and the set of languages in the
    type. Each supported language — `en`, `fr`, `de`, `es`, `pl` — is a value which lifts a
    translation into a single-language `Polyglot`, alternatives combine with `|`, and the
    combined type is the intersection of the languages provided, so a `Polyglot[Text, en & fr]`
    has an English and a French text. Reading one needs a `Locale` of a language it carries,
    and a translation set lacking a required language is a compile error rather than a runtime
    fallback in front of the one user who chose that language.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val greeting: Polyglot[Text, en & fr] = en(t"Hello") | fr(t"Bonjour")

    given Locale[en & fr] = Locale(fr)
    greeting()   // t"Bonjour"
    ```

    A `Locale` usually arrives at runtime, from a header or a setting: a language code decodes
    to `Locale[en & pl & fr & de & es]`, falling back to English for a code it does not know.
    A `Polyglot` may carry any type, not only text — a date format, a currency convention, a
    whole page.

    Each language also has a sort order, its `Collatable` instance, following CLDR: Polish
    and Spanish tailor the Unicode collation (ó after o, ñ after n), while English, French and
    German use the root table. The top-level given `localeCollation` turns the `Locale` in scope
    into the `Collation` that gossamer's text comparison uses; it is a top-level given, so it
    is imported by name (`import soundness.localeCollation`), along with
    `soundness.collationComparable` for ordering `Text` by a collation.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import soundness.collationComparable
    import soundness.localeCollation
    import soundness.sortingAlgorithms.timsort

    given Locale[es] = Locale(es)
    List(t"año", t"anzuelo", t"ano").sort   // List(t"ano", t"anzuelo", t"año")
    ```

    The library has one source set, `cosmopolite.core`, which compiles for every platform.
    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella.

refer cosmopolite.Polyglot
refer cosmopolite.Locale
refer cosmopolite.Language
refer cosmopolite.localeCollation

entry .Language   a natural language, which lifts a translation into a single-language `Polyglot`
  detail
      `Language` is the parent of the language objects `en`, `fr`, `de`, `es` and `pl`. Each has
      a two-letter `#code`, and a type member `#Code` naming its phantom type tag (the trait of
      the same name), so `en.Code` is `en`. Applying a language to a value makes the
      `Polyglot` which carries that value in that language:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val hello: Polyglot[Text, en] = en(t"Hello")
      val greeting: Polyglot[Text, en & fr] = hello | fr(t"Bonjour")
      ```

      The translation is a context function of a `Locale` of the language, so code computing
      it — sorting, formatting — sees that language's locale, whatever locale the caller has.
      A new language is an object extending `Language(code)` with a `type Code`, paired with a
      trait to serve as its tag.
  refer cosmopolite.Polyglot
  entry #code   the language's ISO 639-1 code, such as `t"fr"`
  entry #Code   the phantom type which tags values in this language, such as `fr`
  entry #apply   a translation in this language, as a single-language `Polyglot`
      `fr(t"Bonjour")` is a `Polyglot[Text, fr]`. The argument is evaluated with a `Locale` of
      this language in scope, so a translation which sorts or formats text does so in its own
      language.

entry .Polyglot   a value in several languages, its type recording exactly which
  detail
      A `Polyglot[V, L]` holds a `V` for each language in the intersection `L`: one made by
      `en(…) | fr(…)` is a `Polyglot[V, en & fr]`. Requiring one with a language it lacks does
      not compile, so a missing translation is found where the value is written.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val greeting: Polyglot[Text, en & fr] = en(t"Hello") | fr(t"Bonjour")
      val partial: Polyglot[Text, en & fr] = en(t"Hello")   // does not compile

      given Locale[en & fr] = Locale(fr)
      greeting()   // t"Bonjour"
      ```

      `#apply()` reads the value in the language of the `Locale` in scope, which must be a
      `Locale` of the polyglot's languages.
  refer cosmopolite.Language
  refer cosmopolite.Locale
  entry #values   the translations, keyed by `Language`
  entry #|   combines translations, or picks one at once if a `Locale` for both is in scope
      `a | b` merges two polyglots into one whose language type is the intersection of both.
      It is transparent and inline: when a `Locale` covering both sets of languages is already
      in scope where the expression is written, it reads that locale's value immediately and the
      result is the value itself rather than a `Polyglot`. Where both carry a language, the
      right-hand translation wins.
  entry #apply   the value in the language of the `Locale` in scope
      Needs a `Locale[L]` for the polyglot's language type `L`; since that locale names one of
      the languages the value carries, the read cannot miss.

entry .Locale   the language a program is speaking, as a contextual value
  detail
      A `Locale` holds a `Language`, and its type parameter states which languages it may be:
      a `Locale[en & fr]` is one of English or French, which is what a `Polyglot[V, en & fr]`
      needs to be read. The parameter is contravariant, so a locale known to be French serves
      where any of a set including French is asked for.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given Locale[en & pl & fr & de & es] = t"pl".as[Locale[en & pl & fr & de & es]]
      ```

      A `Locale` in scope also selects its language's sort order through `localeCollation`.
  refer cosmopolite.Polyglot
  refer cosmopolite.localeCollation
  entry .encodable   writes a locale as its language's code, such as `t"fr"`
  entry .inspectable   a debug rendering of a locale, as `Locale(fr)`
      The constructor form, so it is not mistaken for the bare code `.encodable` writes.
  entry .decodable   reads a locale from a language code, falling back to English
      Decodes to `Locale[en & pl & fr & de & es]`: `t"pl"`, `t"fr"`, `t"de"` and `t"es"` are
      their languages, and any other text — including a regional form such as `t"fr-CA"`, or
      upper case — is silently `en`. Normalise the code first if the fallback matters.
  entry #language   the language this locale speaks

entry .Collatable   a typeclass giving a language its sort order, as a `Collation`
  detail
      `pl is Collatable` provides the `Collation` by which Polish text sorts; the subject is the
      language's phantom tag. Each language object holds its own instance as `collatable`, so
      it is in implicit scope. Code does not usually summon it: `localeCollation` turns the
      `Locale` in scope into its language's collation.
  refer cosmopolite.localeCollation
  entry #collation   the language's collation

entry .en   English, the language with code `en`, and its phantom type tag
  detail
      A `Language`: `en(value)` makes a `Polyglot[V, en]`. As a type, `en` tags values and
      locales in English. English sorts by the root Unicode collation, which keeps accented
      letters with their bases (`cafe < café < caff`, `año < anzuelo`).
  entry .Code   the phantom tag of English: `en`
  entry .collatable   English collation: the root Unicode collation table

entry .fr   French, the language with code `fr`, and its phantom type tag
  detail
      A `Language`: `fr(value)` makes a `Polyglot[V, fr]`. As a type, `fr` tags values and
      locales in French. French sorts by the root Unicode collation with forward secondary
      accents (`cote < coté < côte < côté`), as CLDR's `fr` does; the backward accents of
      traditional, now Canadian, French are not used.
  entry .Code   the phantom tag of French: `fr`
  entry .collatable   French collation: the root Unicode table, with forward secondary accents

entry .de   German, the language with code `de`, and its phantom type tag
  detail
      A `Language`: `de(value)` makes a `Polyglot[V, de]`. As a type, `de` tags values and
      locales in German. German sorts by the root Unicode collation, in which umlauts differ
      from their bases only at the secondary level (`ab < äb`, `äa < ab`), as CLDR's standard
      German does; DIN phonebook order is not provided.
  entry .Code   the phantom tag of German: `de`
  entry .collatable   German collation: the root Unicode table, umlauts differing at the secondary level

entry .es   Spanish, the language with code `es`, and its phantom type tag
  detail
      A `Language`: `es(value)` makes a `Polyglot[V, es]`. As a type, `es` tags values and
      locales in Spanish. Spanish sorts with ñ as a letter of its own between n and o, so
      `ano < anzuelo < año`, with Ñ a tertiary (case) variant of ñ.
  entry .Code   the phantom tag of Spanish: `es`
  entry .collatable   Spanish collation: CLDR's, with ñ a letter between n and o

entry .pl   Polish, the language with code `pl`, and its phantom type tag
  detail
      A `Language`: `pl(value)` makes a `Polyglot[V, pl]`. As a type, `pl` tags values and
      locales in Polish. Polish sorts its nine accented letters — ą ć ę ł ń ó ś ź ż — as letters
      in their own right, each after its base, with ż after ź (`z < ź < ż`) and each capital a
      tertiary variant of its small letter.
  entry .Code   the phantom tag of Polish: `pl`
  entry .collatable   Polish collation: CLDR's, with nine accented letters sorted after their bases

entry .via   a value computed in a locale: `Text via pl` is `Locale[pl] ?=> Text`
    An infix type alias for a context function of a `Locale`. A definition typed `T via L` is
    evaluated with a `Locale[L]` in scope, so whatever it computes — a sort, a formatted
    number — follows that locale, and its callers must provide one.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import soundness.collationComparable
    import soundness.localeCollation

    def first: Text via pl = List(t"ó", t"oz").minimum.or(t"")

    given Locale[pl] = Locale(pl)
    first   // t"oz"
    ```

entry .localeCollation   the `Collation` of the `Locale` in scope's language, for sorting and comparing text
    A top-level given, needing a `Locale[L]` and an `L is Collatable`: it provides the
    `Collation` gossamer's text ordering uses, so `sort`, `<` and `minimum` on `Text` follow the
    locale's language. It sits at package level because neither companion can hold it —
    `Collation` belongs to gossamer, the language tags to cosmopolite — so it must be imported
    by name, `import soundness.localeCollation`, alongside `soundness.collationComparable`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import soundness.collationComparable
    import soundness.localeCollation
    import soundness.sortingAlgorithms.timsort

    given Locale[pl] = Locale(pl)
    List(t"pod", t"ó", t"oz").sort   // List(t"oz", t"ó", t"pod")
    ```
